KG42.register("rules-d8c2548721cfdfc04f215c0d",{"id":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation::v141-c-R002","paper":"17-exogenous_cost_allocation","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R002","mechanism_id":"v141-M01","name":"Supply submission","process_stage":"bidding","market_meaning":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power. The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set. The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a). When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set. Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function bidding.supply under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply","coordination.price_signal","matching.counterparties"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E001","source":"bidding.demand","target":"bidding.supply","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E002","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"bidding.supply","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E003","source":"matching.counterparties","target":"bidding.supply","type":"limits","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C003"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"bidding","context_text":"Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). | Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). | Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6). | Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). | Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. | Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration. | Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-nm","symbol":"p_{nm}","meaning":"Bilateral power quantity from agent n to agent m in the local offered-quantity update; its sign is restricted by agent role in Eq. (6a), and it is zero for m outside n's partnership set in Eq. (2).","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued bilateral power quantity over m in omega_n, with p_{nm}=0 for m not in omega_n (Eq. 2)","unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-P-n","symbol":"P_n","meaning":"Vector of agent n's possible bilateral trade proposals, P_n=(p_{nm})_{m in omega_n}, chosen in the local optimization (6a).","kind":"operational","domain":"collection of real-valued bilateral quantities p_{nm} over partners m in omega_n","unit":null,"indices":["n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-n","symbol":"p_n","meaning":"Total amount of power traded by agent n, constrained to equal the sum of its bilateral proposals in the local update (6a).","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued total traded power linked by p_n=sum_{m in omega_n} p_{nm} and bounded by the agent's power boundaries","unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","symbol":"\\omega_n","meaning":"Set of partners of agent n, i.e. agent n's trading partnership set.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of partners of agent n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-nm","symbol":"\\gamma_{nm}","meaning":"Network charge associated with power trade p_{nm} for the given time step, provided a priori by the system operator as an exogenous charge.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given a priori network charge"},"unit":null,"indices":["n","m"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e21","v141-b-g01-e24"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-n0","symbol":"\\gamma_n^0","meaning":"Constant network-charge term that does not depend on the power traded and does not affect the minimization outcome.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given constant network-charge term"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e13","v141-b-g01-e17"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","symbol":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k}","meaning":"Bilateral trade-price signal from iteration k for traded volume p_{nm}; it enters agent n's local offered-quantity update in (6a).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"iteration-k trade-price signal"},"unit":null,"indices":["n","m","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-rho","symbol":"\\rho","meaning":"Positive penalty factor in the consensus-ADMM local objective (6a).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-f-n","symbol":"f_n","meaning":"Cost function of agent n in the local objective; the source states closed, proper, and convex f_n is sufficient for convergence of (6).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given cost function of agent n"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","symbol":"p_{mn}^{k}","meaning":"Partner m's previous bilateral quantity/counterproposal at iteration k that enters agent n's averaging and penalty terms in (6a).","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"iteration-k partner counterproposal quantity"},"unit":null,"indices":["m","n","k"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-underline-p-n","symbol":"\\underline{p}_n","meaning":"Lower power boundary of agent n.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given lower power boundary"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-overline-p-n","symbol":"\\overline{p}_n","meaning":"Upper power boundary of agent n.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"given upper power boundary"},"unit":null,"indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-c","symbol":"\\Omega_c","meaning":"Set of consumers, a subset of the peer-to-peer market community Omega.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"subset of consumers"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-p","symbol":"\\Omega_p","meaning":"Set of prosumers, a subset of the peer-to-peer market community Omega.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"subset of prosumers"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01","v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The partner's previous requested quantity enters the averaging and penalty terms used to update the offered quantity. Scope: Offered-quantity components of the decentralized bilateral negotiation update (6a). Condition: The partner counterproposal represents requested power.","expression":"\\lambda_{nm}^{k}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^{k}-p_{mn}^{k}}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^{k}-p_{mn}^{k}}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","v141-c-R002-theta-rho"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The previous bilateral trade-price signals enter the participant's optimization that updates its offered quantities, economically encouraging consensus with its partners. Scope: Offered-quantity components of participant updates in the decentralized negotiation mechanism (6).","expression":"P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n}\\; f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right]","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-P-n","v141-c-R002-x-p-nm","v141-c-R002-x-p-n"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-nm","v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-n0","v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","v141-c-R002-theta-rho","v141-c-R002-theta-f-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The trading partnership set restricts possible supply offers: the bilateral quantity to a counterparty outside that set must be zero. Scope: Permissible bilateral offered quantities in the P2P trading partnership structure. Condition: Agent m is outside agent n's trading partnership set.","expression":"p_{nm}=0\\quad\\text{if }m\\notin\\omega_n","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["matching.counterparties","bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e09","v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The participant sends its updated offered quantities to the corresponding trading partners. Scope: Submission of offered-quantity components during each decentralized negotiation iteration. Condition: After the participant has updated its trade proposals through its own optimization (6a).","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e20"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"When updating offered quantities, the participant's total power, summed across all its bilateral proposals, must remain within its lower and upper power bounds. Scope: Power feasibility of offered-quantity updates in (6a), at every iteration.","expression":"p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm};\\quad \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le \\overline{p}_n","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-n","v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","v141-c-R002-theta-underline-p-n","v141-c-R002-theta-overline-p-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each bilateral offered quantity must also lie within the prosumer's individual power boundaries. Scope: Bilateral offered-quantity updates by prosumers in (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the prosumer set.","expression":"\\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le \\overline{p}_n\\quad\\text{if }n\\in\\Omega_p","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-underline-p-n","v141-c-R002-theta-overline-p-n","v141-c-R002-theta-omega-p"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e17","v141-b-g01-e18"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Consumers cannot submit positive selling quantities: their bilateral proposal quantities must be nonpositive. Scope: Consumer quantity proposals in the decentralized update (6a). Condition: The submitting participant belongs to the consumer set.","expression":"p_{nm}\\le 0\\quad\\text{if }n\\in\\Omega_c","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["bidding.supply"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R002-x-p-nm"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R002-theta-omega-c"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e10","v141-b-g01-e17"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\mathbf{P}=\\begin{pmatrix} p_{11} & \\cdots & p_{1N_\\Omega} \\\\ \\vdots & & \\vdots \\\\ p_{N_\\Omega 1} & \\cdots & p_{N_\\Omega N_\\Omega} \\end{pmatrix} (2); where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n. Net powers are then obtained by p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm} as in (1c). As outlined in (1b), P is skew-symmetric to insure power balance of each trade, so p_{nn}=0. This allows to potentially individualize prices per trade."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"⎜ ⎟ t (2) P = ⎝ ... ... ... ⎠ wo pNΩ 1 · · · pNΩ NΩ deﬁ where pnm is necessarily equal to zero if ag","equation_id":"Eq. (1e)-(1g)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"In this situation agents can buy a large amount of energy at a low price from one agent to sell it back at a higher price to another. This possibility of arbitraging can be proscribed by limiting the possible amounts traded. Generators, for which p_n\\ge0 and grouped in \\Omega_g, are forbidden to buy power in (1e). On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f). However, prosumers, gathered in \\Omega_p, must still be able to either buy or sell power since they are such that \\underline{p}_n\u003c0\u003c\\overline{p}_n. Power trades of prosumers are bounded by their power boundaries as in (1g)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"a competitive equilibrium which efﬁciency strongly depends on w the chosen network charges. Accordin","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As developed in Appendix B, a decentralized procedure based on the consensus ADMM of [11] is used to solve (5). This decentralized method solves global problem (5) and, hence, leads to a competitive equilibrium which efficiency strongly depends on the chosen network charges. According to [19] ADMM seems well adapted for negotiation mechanisms in smart grids. Several extensions and convergence rate improvements have been proposed in [20]–[23]. Given the focus of this paper is not on scalability a straightforward implementation of consensus ADMM is used. The final decentralized negotiation mechanism reads P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a); s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n; p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a); \\lambda_{nm}^{k+1}=\\lambda_{nm}^k-\\rho(p_{nm}^{k+1}+p_{mn}^{k+1})/2 (6b)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 4, JULY 2019 fn to be closed, proper, and convex is","equation_id":"Eq. (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"where penalty factor \\rho>0. Element \\lambda_{nm} of matrix \\Lambda corresponds to generation price of electricity for traded volume p_{nm}. Possible trades of agents n can be grouped in variable P_n=(p_{nm})_{m\\in\\omega_n}. According to [11], supposing cost functions f_n to be closed, proper, and convex is a sufficient condition to ensure convergence of (6). This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step. However, primal feasibility of trades reciprocity (5b) is only verified at the limit after convergence. Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"on with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to ms all and, when all local resi","equation_id":"Eq. (6)-(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The overall negotiation mechanism occurs as follows. Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n. After receiving all counter proposals (p_{mn}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega}, agent n can compute its new trade prices (\\lambda_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} with (6b), and local residuals (r_n,s_n)^{k+1} with (8). Finally, each agent n broadcasts its local residuals to all and, when all local residuals (r_i,s_i)^{k+1}_{i\\in\\Omega\\setminus\\{n\\}} are received, tests global stopping criteria (7). This process is repeated until convergence. Being decentralized the negotiation mechanism is not supported by a central entity. However, as most information exchanges, a communication standard needs to be defined by an institutional organization to associate a communication protocol between peers participating in the market."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p01-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"p ( ), wi NOMENCLATURE sto to Λ Matrix of trade prices λnm of agent n with agent m con ·T Matrix tra","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Λ Matrix of trade prices λ_nm of agent n with agent m; \\cdot^{T} Matrix transpose operator; γ_nm Network charges of agent n’s trade with agent m; L Set of lines in the power system; N Set of nodes in the power system; | · | Absolute value operator; Ω Peer-to-peer market community of N_Ω agents; Ω_c Set of consumers (subset of Ω); Ω_g Set of generators (subset of Ω); Ω_p Set of prosumers (subset of Ω); ω_n Set of partners of agent n; P Matrix of trade powers p_nm of agent n with agent m; θ_i Voltage angle at node i; \\underline{p}_n/\\overline{p}_n Lower/upper power boundary of agent n; d_nm Electrical distance between agent n and agent m; f_n Cost function of agent n; N^{zone}_{nm} Number of zones between agent n and agent m; p_n Total amount of power traded by agent n; u Network fee of cost allocation policy; Z_il/Y_il Element of bus impedance/admittance matrix from node i to node l."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. P2P Market Design","anchor_text":"t s pu pose. e , egu a at o u ct o h eva uat g et 2) work constraints is be replaced by a cost alloc","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, even though (3) enables the system operator to recover congestion related costs, it does not guarantee the recovery of the costs of maintenance, modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies. This paper proposes to replace the regularization function h with exogenous terms. These exogenous terms would aim not only at allocating congestion-related costs but also costs of maintenance and modernization of power lines, taxes, and policies such as e.g. renewable support schemes. Preference prices as introduced in [18] seems a good candidate for this purpose. Then, regularization function h evaluating network constraints is be replaced by a cost allocation function defined as \\Gamma(\\mathbf{P})=\\sum_{n\\in\\Omega}\\left(\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}\\right) (4); where parameter \\gamma_{nm} is the network charge associated to power trade p_{nm} for the given time step. Constant terms \\gamma_n^0, which do not affect the minimization outcome, allow reflecting costs that are independent of the power traded, such as power line investment and maintenance. Network charges \\gamma_{nm}, detailed in Section III, would then account for congestion-related costs and taxes. Function \\Gamma is separable among participants, and will be integrated in their objective function as it will be further discussed in Section III. Note that \\Gamma also represents the amount of money collected by the system operator from community \\Omega for its use of the power system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e21","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"local residuals. In this context it would be interesting to use a secured mechanism, as does [15] fo","equation_id":"Eq. (4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"When the goal is to obtain a P2P market with allocation of grid-related costs it is possible to use network charges as in (4). Contrary to preference prices chosen by agents, parameters \\gamma_{nm} are provided by the system operator a priori when used as network charges. As mentioned in Section I, remember that, in this paper, a cost allocation policy refers to the way costs are divided between P2P market participants. The proposed cost allocation policies will define how network charges are estimated."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e24","location":{"location_id":"src-12e1fd7a17-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-settlement","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Exogenous Operation Cost Allocation","anchor_text":"whA. Total Fees m. The money paid (resp. received) by agent n for buying if t from (resp. selling to","equation_id":"Eq. (9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The money paid (resp. received) by agent n for buying from (resp. selling to) agent m is given by the perceived price \\mu_n=\\lambda_{nm}-\\gamma_{nm}, as shown in Appendix A. Network charges represent exogenous costs. Thus, when agent n consumes its \\gamma_{nm} are negative which leads to perceived prices \\mu_n higher than trading prices \\lambda_{nm}. When generating perceived prices are lower than trading prices since parameters \\gamma_{nm} would be positive. The total money paid or received by agent n, for real power bilateral trades per time unit, is expressed by \\Lambda_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\lambda_{nm}p_{nm}-\\left(\\underbrace{\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}}_{=\\Gamma_n}\\right), n\\in\\Omega (9); where \\Gamma_n is the part reserved to the system operator."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"bidding.supply","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)+\\frac{\\rho}{2}\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)^2\\right] (6a)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"p_{nm}\\ge0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_g; p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c;"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"P_n^{k+1}=\\arg\\min_{P_n} f_n(p_n)+\\gamma_n^0+\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}\\left[\\gamma_{nm}p_{nm}+\\lambda_{nm}^k\\left(\\frac{p_{nm}^k-p_{mn}^k}{2}-p_{nm}\\right)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","quote":"Note that the additional terms of the augmented Lagrangian in (6a) aims at encouraging, economically, an agent n to reach power consensus with its partners."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e09","quote":"where p_{nm} is necessarily equal to zero if agent m is not in agent n’s trading partnership set \\omega_n."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e20","quote":"Each market participant first solves its own local optimization (6a). Once updated, each agent n individually sends its trade proposals (p_{nm}^{k+1})_{m\\in\\omega} to each partner m\\in\\omega_n."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"s.t. p_n=\\sum_{m\\in\\omega_n}p_{nm}; \\underline{p}_n\\le p_n\\le\\overline{p}_n;"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","quote":"This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"\\underline{p}_n\\le p_{nm}\\le\\overline{p}_n \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_p (6a)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e18","quote":"This formulation allows to have primal feasibility of constraints (5c)–(5g) at each iteration step."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e17","quote":"p_{nm}\\le0 \\text{ if } n\\in\\Omega_c;"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e10","quote":"On the other hand consumers, for which p_n\\le0 and grouped in \\Omega_c, are forbidden to sell power in (1f)."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"c2665a5c08ad850496eb9d39f71d43a655860080f1ebdb5cdfaff40ceedf63b0","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The supply-submission rule has explicit source mathematics in Eq. (6a) and Eq. (2): the local offer update objective, the averaged partner-quantity penalty terms, power-balance and bound constraints, prosumer bilateral bounds, consumer nonpositive offers, and the partnership-set zero restriction. C001-C003 and C005-C007 are formalized with source expressions; C004 remains a procedural submission step because the source gives no formula for the communication action. B evidence is treated only as unreviewed candidate support; no independent source audit is claimed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-nm","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E001","v141-c-R002-E002","v141-c-R002-E003"],"rationale":"p_nm is the bilateral offered-quantity component of the supply submission. Edge E001 carries the partner-quantity averaging input, E002 carries the trade-price-signal input, and E003 restricts p_nm to the partnership set."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-x-P-n","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E001","v141-c-R002-E002","v141-c-R002-E003"],"rationale":"P_n groups the bilateral supply proposals p_nm for m in omega_n. Its index set is the partnership set in E003, while the local update uses the partner-request and price-signal inputs represented by E001 and E002."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-x-p-n","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"p_n is the total traded power obtained as the sum of the supply proposals; it is an aggregate feasibility variable inside the bidding.supply update, not a separate edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-n","node_ids":["matching.counterparties","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E003"],"rationale":"omega_n is the trading-partnership set that limits permissible bilateral supply offers through the matching-to-supply relation E003."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-nm","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E002"],"rationale":"The a priori network charge gamma_nm enters the local supply optimization together with the bilateral price signal on edge E002."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-gamma-n0","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E002"],"rationale":"gamma_n0 is a fixed network-charge constant in the same local objective that receives coordination price signals through E002."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-lambda-nm-k","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E002"],"rationale":"lambda_nm^k is the previous bilateral trade-price signal entering the supply update; this is exactly the coordination-to-supply relation E002."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-rho","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E001"],"rationale":"rho weights the quadratic penalty on the averaged partner-quantity discrepancy; the partner-counterproposal input is represented by E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-f-n","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"f_n is the agent's given cost function in the local supply optimization; it is attached to the supply decision but has no separate graph edge."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-p-mn-k","node_ids":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E001"],"rationale":"p_mn^k is the partner's previous counterproposal quantity entering the supply update; this is the demand-to-supply input relation E001."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-underline-p-n","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The lower power boundary bounds total or prosumer bilateral supply quantities in the local update; no dedicated graph edge represents a bound."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-overline-p-n","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The upper power boundary bounds total or prosumer bilateral supply quantities in the local update; no dedicated graph edge represents a bound."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-c","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Omega_c is the consumer set used to condition the nonpositive supply-offer constraint; the frozen rule has no participation.consumer node, so it is bound to bidding.supply."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-theta-omega-p","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Omega_p is the prosumer set used to condition the bilateral-bound constraint; the frozen rule has no participation.prosumer node, so it is bound to bidding.supply."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","node_ids":["bidding.demand","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E001"],"rationale":"C001 is the averaged partner-quantity input to the supply update, represented by edge E001 from bidding.demand to bidding.supply."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","node_ids":["coordination.price_signal","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E002"],"rationale":"C002 formalizes the price-signal terms in the local supply optimization, represented by edge E002 from coordination.price_signal to bidding.supply."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","node_ids":["matching.counterparties","bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":["v141-c-R002-E003"],"rationale":"C003 is the partnership-set restriction that forces p_nm=0 outside omega_n, exactly the limits relation on edge E003."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C004 is the procedural submission of updated offers to partners. It is retained as a non-formalized action on bidding.supply because the source gives communication prose but no mathematical expression."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C005 formalizes the total-power definition and bounds inside the supply update; it does not add a new cross-node relation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C006","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C006 formalizes the prosumer bilateral bounds on p_nm within the supply update; no separate edge represents these bounds."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C007","node_ids":["bidding.supply"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C007 formalizes nonpositive consumer supply offers within the supply update; no separate edge represents this role restriction."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
